# Observe Agent Turns

This Livebook observes the live result and final settlement of one Agent Turn.
It shows why these are separate events.

## Install Jido

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:jido, path: Path.expand("..", __DIR__)}
])
```

## Define an Agent and an observer

```elixir
defmodule ObserveGuide.Counter do
  use Jido.Agent, name: "observe_guide_counter"

  agent do
    schema Zoi.object(%{count: Zoi.integer() |> Zoi.default(0)})
  end

  routes do
    signal_source "/guides/observe"

    route "counter.add" do
      action %{amount: amount},
        name: "observe_guide_add",
        schema: Zoi.object(%{amount: Zoi.integer()}),
        context: context do
        {:ok, %{context.agent_state | count: context.agent_state.count + amount}}
      end

      define :add, args: [:amount]
    end
  end
end

defmodule ObserveGuide.Forwarder do
  def handle(event, measurements, metadata, receiver) do
    send(receiver, {:jido_event, event, measurements, metadata})
  end
end
```

The handler only sends the bounded event to another process. Telemetry handlers
run in the process that emits the event. Do not call the observed Agent or do
storage or network work in a handler.

## Attach the handler

```elixir
observer = self()
handler_id = "observe-guide-#{System.unique_integer([:positive])}"

:ok =
  :telemetry.attach_many(
    handler_id,
    [
      [:jido, :agent, :turn, :stop],
      [:jido, :agent, :turn, :exception],
      [:jido, :agent, :turn, :settled]
    ],
    &ObserveGuide.Forwarder.handle/4,
    observer
  )
```

## Run one Turn

```elixir
{:ok, _jido} = Jido.start()

{:ok, counter} =
  Jido.start_agent(
    ObserveGuide.Counter,
    id: "observed-counter"
  )

{:ok, committed} = ObserveGuide.Counter.add(counter, 2)
committed.state
```

The Turn `:stop` event reports the live result. On success, its
`state_version_after` revision is already live. The `turn.settled` event reports
the final bounded result after all owned Directive work stops.

This Agent returns no Directives, so the two events occur close together.

```elixir
events =
  for _index <- 1..2 do
    receive do
      {:jido_event, name, measurements, metadata} ->
        %{name: name, measurements: measurements, metadata: metadata}
    after
      5_000 ->
        raise "an Agent Turn event did not arrive"
    end
  end

Enum.map(events, fn event ->
  %{
    name: event.name,
    status: event.metadata.status,
    stage: event.metadata.stage,
    committed?: event.metadata.committed?,
    turn_id: event.metadata.turn_id,
    state_version_before: event.measurements.state_version_before,
    state_version_after: event.measurements[:state_version_after]
  }
end)
```

Both events have the same `turn_id`. For a successful Turn, `committed?` is
`true` and `status` is `:ok`.

For a pre-commit failure, the Turn terminal event has `committed?: false`. No
new revision becomes live. Settlement follows without Directive work.

For a post-commit Directive failure, the Turn `:stop` event remains successful.
The later settlement event has `committed?: true`, `stage: :directive`, and a
non-OK status. The committed revision is not rolled back.

## Read safe fields

The event metadata can include stable Agent identity, activation, Turn, Signal,
trace, status, stage, and safe error fields. Measurements contain integer
durations, revisions, queue sizes, and counts.

The event does not contain Agent state, Plugin-owned state, Signal data, caller
context, process handles, or raw error details.

Use these fields to correlate one Turn:

| Field | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `agent_id` | Logical Agent ID |
| `activation_id` | Current Agent Server activation |
| `turn_id` | One admitted Turn |
| `source_signal_id` | Signal that entered the runtime |
| `signal_id` | Effective Signal selected for execution |
| `trace_id` | Distributed trace |
| `span_id` | Current Turn span |
| `causation_id` | Signal that caused this Signal |

## Detach and stop

```elixir
:ok = :telemetry.detach(handler_id)
:ok = Jido.stop_agent(counter)
:ok = Jido.stop()
```

Read [Telemetry, Tracing, and Logs](telemetry-tracing-and-logs.html) before you
connect a production metric reporter or OpenTelemetry exporter.
